WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.874
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.874 --> 00:00:04.290
COLT MCANLIS: One of the most
common performance questions

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.390
we get with respect to
Google Cloud Storage

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.250
is related to what
type of buckets

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.290
should I use for the
best performance, which

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.640
is a really good question.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.567
When you create
a new GCS bucket,

00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:15.150
one of the first
things you need to do

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.790
is decide what type of
storage class it has.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.160
And while the text
there is descriptive,

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.650
there's not a lot of room to
discuss all the performance

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.550
nuances.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.905
Generally, these classes can
be broken into two categories.

00:00:26.905 --> 00:00:29.400
Nearline and Codeline
are built for content

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.299
which is accessed
infrequently, and thus, latency

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.590
shouldn't really be considered
when fetching from them.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.630
Regional and
multi-regional, however,

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:40.060
are expected to serve
data as fast as possible.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.500
So what do those terms
actually mean, though?

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.870
Well, a regional GCS
bucket guarantees

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.390
that all the data
in that bucket lies

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.430
in a specified region,
which you determine

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.990
when you create the bucket.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.420
This type of location is
really important in situations

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.990
where you know your client are
or on-prem systems are located

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.480
and you want to get your GCS
buckets as close to those

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.370
systems as possible.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.500
Multi-regional storage,
on the other hand,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.330
guarantees two
replicas of your data

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.900
which are geo-diverse,
about 100 miles apart or so.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.400
This results in the
data being moved

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.310
between regions for better
latency and availability.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.320
More importantly is
that multi-regional

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.960
heavily leverages
edge caching and CDNs

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.110
to provide content to the end
users as fast as possible.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.540
To understand how these
systems respond under stress,

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.449
we set up a simple test.

00:01:26.449 --> 00:01:27.990
Upload a two megabyte
file to a bunch

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.573
of regional and
multi-regional buckets

00:01:29.573 --> 00:01:33.390
and fetch that asset with
caching disabled from a VM

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.400
in US west one.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.930
First, there's a
couple of things

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.730
that we can discern
from this data.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.490
For fetches in the same
region or in a sub-region

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.330
of the multi-region,
performance is pretty on par.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.510
However, once we
get across oceans,

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.260
we can see that the
multi-regional buckets

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.887
tend to perform better.

00:01:50.887 --> 00:01:52.470
The reason for this
is that their data

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.590
was duplicated to a region
of the multi-region, which

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.470
provides better access
point and lower latency

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.044
to our fetching client.

00:02:00.044 --> 00:02:01.710
What these tests show
us is that there's

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.200
no really specific
performance different

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.690
of the classifications of
the buckets themselves.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.669
Rather, the performance
is dominated

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:12.060
by the latency of physics of
the distance between the clients

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.740
and the cloud storage bucket.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.590
However, to be clear,
with caching on,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.320
your read performance for both
regional and multi-regional

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.520
should look something like this.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.030
So if caching is on,
Google's powerful CDN

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.830
can help improve your
read performance,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.240
regardless of the bucket.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.460
But if caching is
off, then it's best

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.410
to pick a class that
puts your content as

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.601
close to your
endpoints as possible.

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:35.850
To learn more about the perf
details of these buckets,

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.290
check out the article linked
in the description below.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.414
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:02:40.414 --> 00:02:41.669
Performance Atlas content.

00:02:41.669 --> 00:02:43.210
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.850
every millisecond counts.

